---
/**
 * AskExamples — worked queries against the real endpoints.
 *
 * Layout follows aiscientist.tools' "What can you ask?" band, and it is a CAROUSEL, not a
 * tab set. That distinction is the whole reason this was rebuilt. The reference renders a
 * single card, remounts it on every change so its entrance animation replays, advances it
 * on a timer with a progress bar across the top, and offers pills, chevrons and dots as
 * three ways into the same index. Implemented as WAI-ARIA tabs with `hidden` panels — as
 * this was — none of that is reachable: a `display: none` panel cannot animate, and a
 * reserved stage height sized to the tallest panel leaves the shortest one sitting in a
 * pool of dead space.
 *
 * Three structural consequences worth knowing before editing:
 *
 * 1. Cards are stacked in ONE grid cell (`grid-area: 1/1`), so the frame is exactly as
 *    tall as the tallest card and no magic reserve is needed. The previous `min-height:
 *    37rem` was a measured guess that still left ~115px of void under the shortest panel.
 * 2. Inactive cards are hidden with `visibility`, never `hidden`/`display: none` — a
 *    display-none card contributes nothing to grid sizing, which would collapse the frame
 *    and reintroduce the jump. Be exact about what that costs, because this comment used
 *    to claim the opposite and the claim survived several reviews: `visibility: hidden`
 *    DOES remove a card from the accessibility tree, so only the showing card is ever
 *    exposed to assistive tech. That is what the WAI-ARIA carousel pattern does rather than
 *    a defect, but be equally exact about what the fix for it does, or this comment repeats
 *    its own history in the other direction. The LABELS carry all of it: the stack's name
 *    ("Worked queries: 4 examples") and each slide's ("2 of 4: Media & metabolism") are what
 *    tell a listener there are four and which one they have. `aria-roledescription` only
 *    changes how the role is spoken and adds no navigation whatsoever; `aria-controls` is
 *    correctness with weak support, not a route. So: the three cards that are not showing
 *    are ABSENT from the accessibility tree, and every reader who wants them gets there
 *    through the pills, which are labelled buttons and always were.
 * 3. The entrance is TRANSFORM-ONLY. The old tab fade animated opacity and justified it on
 *    the grounds that an accessibility audit never clicks a tab. Auto-advance destroys that
 *    argument — an audit can now sample a card mid-fade, and axe computes contrast through
 *    opacity, with `docs.yml` gating deploys on Lighthouse accessibility ≥ 0.90. Same
 *    constraint, same resolution as styles/reveal.css.
 * 4. Rotation is BOUNDED, and starts when the band is first on screen. It used to start at
 *    page load several screens above here, so a reader arriving at the band landed on an
 *    arbitrary card behind a half-drawn progress bar and never saw the first one; and it
 *    then advanced every 16s for as long as the tab stayed open. It now makes three passes
 *    over the set and rests, holds while the pointer or focus is inside, and carries an
 *    explicit stop button, because hover is not a mechanism a keyboard or screen-reader
 *    user has and WCAG 2.2.2 asks for one they do.
 *
 * Two deliberate departures from the reference.
 *
 * No "Tools used" row. Theirs is real — their answers come from executing tools. CAAIL's
 * come from fetching static JSON, so a tool-call trace would be a staged prop. The source
 * row names the actual endpoint instead, which is both true and more useful to anyone
 * about to query it themselves.
 *
 * The questions are written at the specificity a PhD would actually type: a named method
 * against a named problem, with the artifact they need at the end of it. "What AI work
 * exists on scaffolding" is a browsing prompt, not a research question, and answering it
 * well tells a computational biologist nothing they could act on.
 *
 * Every figure is computed from the generated API at build time. Assumptions that stop
 * holding throw here rather than misleading on the page.
 */
import { Icon } from 'astro-icon/components';
import matrix from '../../public/api/matrix.json';
import papers from '../../public/api/papers.json';
import datasetsApi from '../../public/api/datasets.json';
import catalog from '../../public/api/catalog.json';
import counts from '../content/data/counts.json';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');

type Cell = (typeof matrix.cells)[number];

const byId = new Map(papers.references.map((r) => [r.id, r]));
const cite = (id: number): string => {
  const r = byId.get(id);
  if (!r) return `#${id}`;
  const who = (r.authors?.[0] ?? '').split(',')[0] || 'Unknown';
  return `${who}${r.year ? ` ${r.year}` : ''} (#${id})`;
};

/** The named method row a reader would name in a question. */
const mediaBo = matrix.cells.find((c) => c.method === 'Bayesian Optimization' && c.area === 'media')!;
if (!mediaBo) throw new Error('AskExamples: Bayesian Optimization × media cell missing from matrix.json');

/* Populations, each labelled by what it counted.

   This used to render one figure: papers + software + databases + datasets, presented as
   "N curated resources". That sum overstates curation in two directions at once. The paper
   total is every entry across all six sections, not the matrix-eligible subset, and the
   dataset total is mostly per-study inventory rows rather than curated entries. Summing
   four populations that were counted by four different rules produces a number about
   nothing, and it is the largest number on the band, so it is the one a reader carries away.

   CLAUDE.md states the rule directly: "345 papers" and "papers in the matrix" are different
   numbers, so label every count with the population it counted. The lede names the parts and
   no longer offers a total.

   Every figure is read from the same endpoints an agent would read, and the two partitions
   are asserted below. A hand-typed number beside a machine-derived one is this repo's most
   expensive recurring defect; the mitigation is to derive it or to fail when the two
   disagree, and this does both. */
const primaryPapers = papers.references.filter((r) => r.isPrimary).length;
const reviewPapers = papers.references.filter((r) => r.section === 'Reviews & Perspectives').length;
const referenceWorkPapers = papers.references.length - primaryPapers - reviewPapers;
const curatedDatasets = datasetsApi.entries.length;
const inventoryRows = datasetsApi.inventory.length;

/* Partition guards. These fire at build time, so a section rename in Papers.md or a new
   dataset page shape breaks the build instead of quietly reallocating readers between two
   populations the page names separately. */
if (primaryPapers + reviewPapers + referenceWorkPapers !== counts.papers) {
  throw new Error(
    `AskExamples: paper populations do not partition: ${primaryPapers} primary + ` +
      `${reviewPapers} reviews + ${referenceWorkPapers} reference-work ≠ ${counts.papers} total`,
  );
}
if (curatedDatasets + inventoryRows !== counts.datasets) {
  throw new Error(
    `AskExamples: dataset populations do not partition: ${curatedDatasets} curated + ` +
      `${inventoryRows} inventory ≠ ${counts.datasets} total`,
  );
}

/* ---- Card 1: the bovine substrate a planned timecourse would have to join ----------------
   Every figure below is read from datasets.json rather than named, because the whole point of
   the card is that the inventory is deeper than a browsing reader would guess. `columns` exists
   only on inventory rows (curated entries have no table), hence the optional access. */
type InventoryRow = { page: string; columns?: Record<string, string> };
const cowInventory = (datasetsApi.inventory as InventoryRow[]).filter((r) => r.page === 'Cow');
const cowCurated = datasetsApi.entries.filter((e) => e.page === 'Cow');
const cowTotal = cowInventory.length + cowCurated.length;
const col = (r: InventoryRow, k: string) => r.columns?.[k] ?? '';
const acc = (r: InventoryRow) => col(r, 'Data').replace(/\[`|`\]\(.*/g, '');

/* This card said the opposite until CAAIL-258 landed, and the reversal is the point.
   `GSE173199` is a GEO SuperSeries, and CAAIL indexed only the parent, so the bulk
   0/24/48/72/96 h timecourse inside it was unreachable from the endpoint. The card
   concluded from that silence that the only real timecourse was single-nucleus and told
   the reader to copy its grid. A ten-minute GEO search returned the right answer the whole
   time. Now that the members are indexed, the honest advice inverts: the grid is already
   public at n=4 under serum starvation, so the 12 libraries are worth more under the
   trigger that has no replicated series.

   Each deposit is matched on its DESIGN rather than its accession, so a re-deposit under a
   new accession does not silently drop a row, and each guard below names what it protects.

   A parent SuperSeries row now DESCRIBES its members, including their grid and replication, so
   a filter for the timecourse matches the parent too. `.find()` then returns whichever comes
   first in table order, which is the parent, and the card renders the accession that resolves
   to nothing analysable. That is how this card was wrong the first time, arriving by a new
   route: the fix to the parent's description armed it. So every lookup here EXCLUDES SuperSeries
   parents and asserts it matched exactly one row, rather than trusting the first hit. */
const only = (rows: InventoryRow[], what: string): InventoryRow => {
  if (rows.length !== 1) {
    throw new Error(
      `AskExamples card 1: expected exactly one ${what} in the Cow inventory, found ` +
        `${rows.length}${rows.length ? ` (${rows.map(acc).join(', ')})` : ''}. A .find() here ` +
        `would have silently taken the first.`,
    );
  }
  return rows[0]!;
};
const notSuperSeries = (r: InventoryRow) => !/superseries/i.test(col(r, 'Description'));
const cowBulkTimecourse = only(
  cowInventory.filter(
    (r) => notSuperSeries(r)
      && /^rna-seq/i.test(col(r, 'Type'))
      && /0\/24\/48\/72\/96/.test(col(r, 'Description'))
      && /quadruplicate/i.test(col(r, 'Description')),
  ),
  'bulk quadruplicate timecourse',
);
/* The serum-free arm: bulk, but two points rather than a series. */
const cowSerumFreeArm = only(
  cowInventory.filter(
    (r) => notSuperSeries(r)
      && /^rna-seq/i.test(col(r, 'Type'))
      && /serum-free arm/i.test(col(r, 'Description')),
  ),
  'bulk serum-free arm',
);
/* The one deposit that does cover the full grid under serum-free induction, and cannot be
   pooled with: a single multiplexed single-nucleus library, so n=1 at every timepoint. */
const cowSingleNucleus = only(
  cowInventory.filter(
    (r) => notSuperSeries(r)
      && /snrna-seq/i.test(col(r, 'Type'))
      && /0\/24\/48\/72\/96/.test(col(r, 'Description')),
  ),
  'single-nucleus deposit on the same grid',
);
/* The card's checkable catch: the obvious regulatory reference cannot actually be used yet.
   If the deposit opens up this throws rather than shipping a stale warning. */
const cowEmbargoed = cowInventory.find((r) => /embargoed/i.test(col(r, 'Description')));
if (!cowEmbargoed) {
  throw new Error(
    'AskExamples card 1: the embargoed annotation reference is gone from the Cow inventory, so ' +
      'the "check before you rely on it" row no longer has a subject.',
  );
}
/* The three deposits must be three DIFFERENT rows. Two of the filters resolving to one row would
   render the same accession twice and read as a contradiction rather than a comparison. */
if (new Set([cowBulkTimecourse, cowSerumFreeArm, cowSingleNucleus].map(acc)).size !== 3) {
  throw new Error(
    'AskExamples card 1: the timecourse, the serum-free arm and the single-nucleus deposit did ' +
      `not resolve to three distinct accessions (${acc(cowBulkTimecourse)}, ` +
      `${acc(cowSerumFreeArm)}, ${acc(cowSingleNucleus)}).`,
  );
}
/* The card's whole argument is that the serum-free side has no replicated series. If one is
   ever deposited, the advice flips back and the card has to be rewritten rather than quietly
   continuing to send readers at the thin arm. */
if (/triplicate|quadruplicate/i.test(col(cowSerumFreeArm, 'Description'))
    && /0\/24\/48\/72\/96/.test(col(cowSerumFreeArm, 'Description'))) {
  throw new Error(
    'AskExamples card 1: the serum-free arm now covers the full grid with replication, so ' +
      '"spend your libraries there" is no longer the advice. Rewrite the card.',
  );
}

/* ---- Card 2: where the model-plus-loop work is actually filed ----------------------------
   This card previously led on "none of the 10 is ALSO tagged metabolic modeling, and all 10 are
   tagged, so the gap is in their content and not in our coverage". That verification does not hold
   and the claim has been removed.

   Two reasons it fails. Topic tags mark a paper's PRIMARY SUBJECT, not the methods it uses: the
   27 papers tagged metabolic-modeling are GEM reconstructions and flux-balance work, so asking
   whether a media-optimization paper is "also tagged metabolic modeling" asks whether it is
   primarily a metabolic-modeling paper, which is close to tautologically no. And tagging is far
   too sparse to carry an absence claim: 53 papers in the corpus have no tags at all and 131 have
   exactly one, so "every paper here is tagged" is satisfied by a single obvious label and says
   nothing about what was considered. Ref #249 couples solution thermodynamics to a BO media loop
   and would have falsified the old headline on its own.

   The rebuild then smuggled the same error back in a new form: it filtered on `methods`, which
   116 of 345 references do not carry, because it is populated only for matrix-eligible primary
   research. Ref #240 is the asker's question answered (the first genome-scale model for Sus
   scrofa, constrained with measured biomass composition and uptake fluxes, validated by
   supplementation) and it was invisible to that filter by construction, being filed as a
   reference work.

   So this card now derives from `title` and `section`, both of which every reference carries.
   The finding it reports is that structural fact rather than a claim about the field: the matrix
   reaches a fraction of the genome-scale work, because most of it is not method-to-area
   research. That is arithmetic over a total field and cannot be wrong the way a superlative can. */
const gemPapers = papers.references.filter((r) =>
  /genome-scale|metabolic network|metabolic model/i.test(r.title),
);
const gemInMatrix = gemPapers.filter((r) => (r.methods ?? []).length > 0);
/* The one that answers the question as asked, matched on what makes it that rather than by id. */
const gemMediaDesign = gemPapers.find((r) => /media design|media formulation/i.test(r.title));
const mediaBoCode = papers.references.filter((r) => mediaBo.refIds.includes(r.id) && r.hasCode);
const mediaBoBoth = papers.references.filter(
  (r) => mediaBo.refIds.includes(r.id) && r.hasCode && r.hasData,
);
if (!gemMediaDesign || gemInMatrix.length >= gemPapers.length) {
  throw new Error(
    'AskExamples card 2: expected a genome-scale media-design paper and genome-scale work ' +
      `filed outside the matrix; got mediaDesign=${!!gemMediaDesign} ` +
      `inMatrix=${gemInMatrix.length} of ${gemPapers.length}`,
  );
}

/* ---- Card 3: allergenicity screening for a novel-food submission -------------------------
   This slot held a coverage card reading the matrix as a map of the field. It was cut, and the
   reason is worth keeping: CAAIL's curation is mid-flight (new matrix columns and a placement
   re-audit are both open), so a card whose entire subject is coverage asserts something that is
   still moving, and a scope hedge cannot rescue a claim that IS the scope.

   What replaced it names things a reader opens instead of counting the corpus. Every row below
   is a list drawn from `catalog.json` groups, so the card cannot claim a tool exists that the
   catalog does not carry, and adding one to Databases.md puts it on the homepage automatically.

   The reasoning the card contributes, which a search does not: these tools look interchangeable
   and are not. One implements the FAO/WHO Codex criterion itself; the rest are classifiers
   answering "allergen-like" against different training sets, so agreement between them is not
   confirmation. Deliberately no claim about what any regulator accepts. */
type CatalogEntry = { name: string; url: string; group: string };
const inGroup = (list: CatalogEntry[], group: string) => list.filter((e) => e.group === group);
const allergenTools = inGroup(catalog.software as CatalogEntry[], 'Food Safety & Allergenicity');
const allergenDbs = inGroup(catalog.databases as CatalogEntry[], 'Food Safety & Allergen Databases');
/* The screen that is the criterion rather than a model of it. */
const codexScreen = allergenTools.find((e) => /Allermatch/i.test(e.name));
/* The precedent archive, and the row nobody expects: every cultured-animal-cell food that has
   completed an FDA pre-market consultation, with the sponsor's own safety submission attached. */
const culturedCellPrecedent = (catalog.databases as CatalogEntry[]).find((e) =>
  /Cultured Animal Cells/i.test(e.name),
);
const classifiers = allergenTools.filter((e) => !/Allermatch/i.test(e.name));
if (!codexScreen || !culturedCellPrecedent || classifiers.length < 3 || allergenDbs.length < 3) {
  throw new Error(
    'AskExamples card 3: expected the Codex screen, the FDA cultured-cell consultation ' +
      'inventory, 3+ other allergenicity classifiers and 3+ allergen reference databases; got ' +
      `codex=${!!codexScreen} precedent=${!!culturedCellPrecedent} ` +
      `classifiers=${classifiers.length} databases=${allergenDbs.length}`,
  );
}

/* ---- Card 4: sensory, and what a small-n asker can actually build on ----------------------
   The head used to be "40 samples is not the shortfall it looks like". The corpus does not
   support that and ref #72 measured the opposite: a subsampling analysis from 50 to 170 training
   samples with 39 held out, on 209 total, with accuracy still rising across the range. So the
   card keeps the useful half (the sensory literature is classical and small-n friendly) and stops
   telling the reader their sample count is fine.

   The regex here names FOUR rows and must keep matching four. The previous one included `Linear`,
   which silently pulled in a fifth row (Linear & Regularized Models) and turned the prose's 32
   into a rendered 34 that no sentence on the card accounted for. The guard below pins that. */
const sensoryCells = matrix.cells
  .filter((c) => c.area === 'sensory' && !c.emptyInCorpus)
  .sort((a, b) => b.refIds.length - a.refIds.length);
const sensoryTop = sensoryCells[0];
const sensoryDeep = sensoryCells.filter((c) => /Deep Learning|CNN|GNN/i.test(c.method));
const sensoryClassical = sensoryCells.filter((c) =>
  /Ensemble|SVM|Chemometrics|Nearest Neighbors/i.test(c.method),
);
const nRefs = (cs: Cell[]) => new Set(cs.flatMap((c) => c.refIds)).size;
const sensoryIds = new Set(sensoryCells.flatMap((c) => c.refIds));
const sensoryRefs = papers.references.filter((r) => sensoryIds.has(r.id));
/* Code release, on MATCHED populations. The earlier version compared sensory against all 345,
   which folds in 116 reviews and reference-work entries that structurally cannot carry a repo,
   so it understated the corpus baseline and made sensory look normal. Every sensory reference is
   primary research (asserted below), so the comparison group is the rest of the primary corpus. */
const sensoryCode = sensoryRefs.filter((r) => r.hasCode).length;
const nonSensoryPrimary = papers.references.filter((r) => r.isPrimary && !sensoryIds.has(r.id));
const nonSensoryCode = nonSensoryPrimary.filter((r) => r.hasCode).length;
const pct = (a: number, b: number) => Math.round((100 * a) / b);
/* The one paper in the column that measured the sample-count question the asker is asking, so
   the card cites it rather than asserting a floor of its own. Derived by title, not by ref id:
   an id typed into prose is the same hand-typed-fact defect as a typed count. */
const sensoryLearningCurve = sensoryRefs.find((r) => /metabolomic selection/i.test(r.title));
if (!sensoryLearningCurve) {
  throw new Error(
    'AskExamples card 4: the sample-count row cites the one sensory paper that ran a training-set ' +
      'subsampling analysis, and it is no longer in the column. Recheck the row before shipping.',
  );
}
if (!sensoryTop || sensoryClassical.length !== 4 || nRefs(sensoryClassical) <= nRefs(sensoryDeep)) {
  throw new Error(
    'AskExamples card 4: the card names four classical method rows and rests on them ' +
      `outnumbering the deep rows; got rows=${sensoryClassical.length} ` +
      `classical=${nRefs(sensoryClassical)} deep=${nRefs(sensoryDeep)}`,
  );
}
if (!sensoryRefs.every((r) => r.isPrimary)) {
  throw new Error(
    'AskExamples card 4: a sensory reference is no longer primary research, so comparing its ' +
      'code-release rate against the rest of the primary corpus is no longer like for like.',
  );
}

/* The licensing card that stood here was cut. Its central row said "what your organisation may
   lawfully read, it may generally index for itself", and the statutes say otherwise for the
   reader it addressed: UK CDPA s.29A permits text and data mining only for non-commercial
   research, and EU DSM Art. 3 is limited to research organisations (Art. 2(1): not-for-profit or
   public-interest), so a company falls to Art. 4, which carries a machine-readable opt-out and is
   the one exception Art. 7(1) leaves contractually overridable. Nothing replaces it: a homepage
   card is the wrong place to hold a jurisdictional position, and a corrected sentence would still
   be one. The licence tiers remain available at /licenses/ where they carry their own caveats. */

/* FOUR questions, each answerable only by a tool call PLUS judgement, and each drawing on a
   different slice so no two are answerable from the same part of the corpus: bovine deposits,
   the paper corpus read across its sections, the software and database catalog, and the matrix.

   A note on what is NOT here. An earlier version credited "all 112 entries in GFI's solutions
   database". That figure was hand-typed, it sat nine words after "nothing here is illustrative",
   and the page does not support it: gfi.org/solutions advertises 45+ research opportunities,
   50+ commercial whitespaces and 35+ ecosystem needs, and states no total. It is gone rather
   than corrected, because a number nobody can check is worth less than the sentence it cost.

   VOICE: every asker already has cells, data and expertise. They are not being introduced to
   the field, and no card explains cellular agriculture to them. Q3 is a team preparing a
   submission rather than an individual at a bench, which is why its pill reads differently. */
const examples = [
  {
    id: 'design',
    tab: 'Designing to integrate',
    icon: 'lucide:dna',
    label: 'Experiment design × existing deposits',
    q:
      'I am planning an RNA-seq timecourse on primary bovine satellite cells through differentiation, ' +
      'budget for about 12 libraries. I would like this to be integrable with existing data rather ' +
      'than standing alone. Which public datasets could mine realistically be combined with, and what ' +
      'do I need to match in the design (timepoints, depth, annotation build) for that to be possible?',
    src: ['datasets.json'],
    head: 'The grid you were going to spend 12 libraries on is already public. What is thin is the trigger you would be spending them under',
    rows: [
      { k: 'Already public, at full grid', v: `${acc(cowBulkTimecourse)} is bulk RNA-seq at 0/24/48/72/96 h in biological quadruplicates, with differentiation induced by serum starvation. Repeating that grid under that trigger buys you replication, not reach` },
      { k: 'Where the public grid is thin', v: `Under serum-free induction there is no comparable series. ${acc(cowSerumFreeArm)} is bulk but samples only 0 h and 72 h, and ${acc(cowSingleNucleus)} covers all five timepoints in a single multiplexed single-nucleus library` },
      { k: 'What you actually have to match', v: 'The induction trigger, not the timing. Match serum starvation and you can pool with the existing series directly. Choose serum-free and your 12 libraries become the first replicated timecourse under that trigger, which is the more valuable of the two things you could buy' },
      { k: 'Check before you rely on it', v: `The bovine FAANG epigenome atlas is the obvious annotation reference and its new deposits are embargoed until journal acceptance` },
    ],
    tail:
      'Both arms are deposited inside one parent accession that names neither, so a list of deposits ' +
      'will not tell you this and the design lines will. Read the thin side as thin in what is ' +
      `indexed here: CAAIL holds ${cowTotal} bovine datasets as a curated subset and has not ` +
      'measured its own recall, so this is not evidence that nobody has run it.',
  },
  {
    id: 'media',
    tab: 'Media & metabolism',
    icon: 'lucide:flask-conical',
    label: 'Media optimization × metabolic modeling',
    q:
      'We are trying to cut the cost of our serum-free medium and have reached the point where ' +
      'one-factor-at-a-time screening has stopped paying. I have a draft genome-scale model for our ' +
      'species but no experience closing the loop between a model and a screen. What work couples a ' +
      'metabolic model to an experimental design loop, and what did those groups actually have to ' +
      'measure to make the model useful?',
    src: ['matrix.json', 'papers.json'],
    head: `${gemPapers.length} papers here are genome-scale or metabolic-network work. The matrix reaches ${gemInMatrix.length} of them`,
    rows: [
      { k: 'The cell you would search', v: `Bayesian Optimization × Media Optimization holds ${mediaBo.refIds.length} papers. ${mediaBoCode.length} link released code, and ${mediaBoBoth.length} of the ${mediaBo.refIds.length} links both code and a data deposit, which is what re-running a loop yourself requires` },
      { k: 'Where the rest of it sits', v: `The other ${gemPapers.length - gemInMatrix.length} carry no matrix method at all, because they are filed as reference works and reviews rather than as method-to-area research. Searching the matrix will not surface them` },
      { k: 'The closest answer to your question', v: `${cite(gemMediaDesign.id)}, "${gemMediaDesign.title}", constrains its model with experimentally determined biomass composition and uptake and excretion fluxes from a muscle satellite-cell line, then validates it by amino-acid supplementation. That is the measurement burden you asked about. It is a preprint and has not been peer reviewed` },
    ],
    tail:
      'What each group had to measure is in their methods sections rather than in any metadata ' +
      'field, which is why this ends in papers to read rather than in a filter. Read the scope ' +
      'precisely: this is where the coupling is indexed, not a claim about who has done it.',
  },
  {
    id: 'safety',
    tab: 'Screening for a submission',
    icon: 'lucide:shield-check',
    label: 'Allergenicity × novel-food dossier',
    q:
      'We have engineered a bovine line expressing a couple of non-native proteins and we are ' +
      'scoping a novel-food submission. Before I commission wet-lab allergenicity work, what can I ' +
      'screen the sequences against computationally, and has anyone in cultivated meat already been ' +
      'through this whose submission I can read?',
    src: ['catalog.json'],
    head: `Only one of these implements the Codex criterion itself. The other ${classifiers.length} score allergen similarity by other means`,
    rows: [
      { k: 'The screen that is the criterion', v: `${codexScreen.name} implements the FAO/WHO Codex Alimentarius test directly: a sliding 80-mer window at or above 35% identity, plus an exact 6-mer match, run against curated allergen sets` },
      { k: 'The classifiers, which answer "allergen-like"', v: `${classifiers.map((e) => e.name).join(', ')}. Alignment-free, homology-plus-structure and deep-learning methods, each trained on a different set, so agreement between them is not confirmation` },
      { k: 'What they are all standing on', v: `${allergenDbs.map((e) => e.name).join(', ')}. Which reference set you screen against moves the answer more than which classifier you run` },
      { k: 'Who has already been through it', v: `${culturedCellPrecedent.name} publishes, per product, the file number, the sponsor's own safety submission and the agency's response` },
    ],
    tail:
      'The screens are cheap, and the choice that matters is the reference set rather than the ' +
      'classifier. What none of them settles is whether your specific protein needs serum-IgE work, ' +
      'which is the spend you were scoping. CAAIL links what each resource says about itself, so ' +
      'check scope and update dates at the source before relying on one.',
  },
  {
    id: 'sensory',
    tab: 'Flavor & sensory',
    icon: 'lucide:donut',
    label: 'Sensory prediction × small-n reality',
    q:
      'We run a small cultured-pork line and have GC-MS volatile panels plus trained-panel scores ' +
      'for about 40 samples, nowhere near enough to train on. Before I commit to another year of ' +
      'panel sessions, which approaches have actually predicted sensory outcomes from instrumental ' +
      'measurements in meat, and what did those groups need in sample counts and reference ' +
      'standards to make it work?',
    src: ['matrix.json', 'papers.json'],
    head: 'The methods are settled and small-n friendly. What is thin is anything you could build on',
    rows: [
      { k: 'What the field actually uses', v: `${nRefs(sensoryClassical)} papers across the ensemble, SVM, chemometrics and nearest-neighbour rows, against ${nRefs(sensoryDeep)} on deep learning, CNN and GNN combined. The largest single cell in the matrix is ${sensoryTop.method} × Sensory Prediction at ${sensoryTop.refIds.length} papers, so your problem is not the architecture` },
      { k: 'What those methods needed in samples', v: `${cite(sensoryLearningCurve.id)} ran your question as a subsampling analysis: training sets from 50 to 170 with 39 held out, on 209 samples, accuracy still climbing across that range though some traits were already usable at 50. The smallest configuration it reports is about 90 samples` },
      { k: 'Why you are starting the baseline, not joining one', v: `Sensory papers release code at ${sensoryCode} of ${sensoryRefs.length} (${pct(sensoryCode, sensoryRefs.length)}%), against ${nonSensoryCode} of ${nonSensoryPrimary.length} (${pct(nonSensoryCode, nonSensoryPrimary.length)}%) across the rest of the primary literature. Every sensory reference is primary research, so that is like for like` },
    ],
    tail:
      'What each group had to measure is in their methods sections, which is the reading this ends ' +
      'in. The useful read is not that 40 samples is fine, because the one paper that measured it ' +
      'says otherwise. It is that the methods you would use are the cheap ones, and the panel ' +
      'sessions are buying the thing the literature will not hand you.',
  },
];---

<section class="ask" id="ask">
  <div class="hd sr">
    {/* Doubles as the HomeRail label for this section — keep the two identical, and see
        the note in HomeRail.astro for why the rail derives from the eyebrow rather than
        from the h2. Guarded by homepage-agent.spec.ts. */}
    <p class="eyebrow"><span class="bar" aria-hidden="true"></span>What you can ask</p>
    <h2 class="caail-display">Ask something specific</h2>
    <p class="lede">
      Real questions against the real endpoints. Behind them: {primaryPapers} primary-research
      papers in the matrix, plus {reviewPapers} reviews and {referenceWorkPapers} reference-work
      chapters; {counts.software} tools and {counts.databases} databases; and {curatedDatasets}
      curated dataset entries over {inventoryRows} per-study deposits. Indexed across
      {matrix.methods.length} AI methods and {matrix.areas.length} research areas.
    </p>
    <p class="sub">
      These are what the endpoints return, not finished answers; your agent reasons on top of
      them. Every figure is read from the corpus when this page is built, so nothing here is
      illustrative.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="pills sr sr-d1">
    {examples.map((e, i) => (
      <button type="button" class="pill" data-pill aria-controls="ask-stack" aria-pressed={i === 0 ? 'true' : 'false'}>
        <Icon name={e.icon} />{e.tab}
      </button>
    ))}
  </div>

  {/* Announces the change for screen readers, which see neither the pill state nor the
      card swap. Deliberately announces the short label, not the four-line question —
      re-reading a paragraph on every advance is punishing to listen to.

      Written ONLY on a change the reader asked for. The timer used to write here too, so
      anyone listening to any part of this page was interrupted every 16 seconds for as
      long as the tab stayed open, whether or not they had ever been near the band.

      A deliberate DEPARTURE from the WAI-ARIA carousel pattern, not an implementation of
      it, and the difference matters to anyone who later tries to close the gap. The pattern
      puts `aria-live` on the slide wrapper itself — `off` while rotating, `polite` once it
      is not — which here would read a ~200-word card aloud end to end on every change. This
      announces a short label from a region of its own instead, and only on a change the
      reader made. Same principle, since nothing automatic is announced either way; not the
      same mechanism. */}
  <p class="vh" aria-live="polite" aria-atomic="true" data-live></p>

  {/* Named and role-described so the one exposed card is announced as part of something,
      rather than as a stray group with no context. `aria-roledescription` is spoken only if
      the element also has a role and an accessible name, hence all three attributes.
      Its reach is narrow and worth stating: it renames the role and does nothing else. It
      does not expose the hidden cards, and it gives nobody a way to move between them. The
      count in this label is what carries that, which is why the label states one. */}
  <div
    class="stack sr sr-d2"
    id="ask-stack"
    role="group"
    aria-roledescription="carousel"
    aria-label={`Worked queries: ${examples.length} examples`}
  >
    {/* Full width with no JS, where it simply reads as the card's accent edge. With JS it
        animates 0 → 1 across the dwell, and its `animationend` is what advances the
        carousel — one clock instead of two, so pausing the animation pauses the advance
        with no elapsed-time bookkeeping to drift. */}
    <div class="prog" data-prog aria-hidden="true"></div>

    {examples.map((e, i) => (
      <article
        class="card"
        data-card
        data-off={i === 0 ? null : ''}
        role="group"
        aria-roledescription="slide"
        aria-label={`${i + 1} of ${examples.length}: ${e.tab}`}
      >
        <div class="chead">
          <p class="plabel"><Icon name={e.icon} />{e.label}</p>
          <p class="q">{e.q}</p>
        </div>

        <div class="csrc">
          <span>Answered from</span>
          {e.src.map((s) => <code>{s}</code>)}
        </div>

        <div class="cbody">
          <p class="head">{e.head}</p>
          <ul class="rows">
            {e.rows.map((r) => (<li><strong>{r.k}</strong><span>{r.v}</span></li>))}
          </ul>
          <p class="tail">{e.tail}</p>
        </div>
      </article>
    ))}
  </div>

  <div class="ctrls sr sr-d3">
    {/* The rotation control, first, as the WAI-ARIA carousel pattern places it. WCAG 2.2.2
        wants a mechanism to stop content that moves by itself, and the two this band had —
        pause on hover, and stop on using any other control — are both mechanisms a reader
        has to already possess a pointer, or already be pressing something, to reach. This
        is the one that is simply there and says what it does. Rendered stopped, because at
        load it is: rotation does not begin until the band is scrolled to.
        Two icons swapped by `display`, which is the `.setup .copy` shape — see the margin
        note beside `.ask .rotate` in starlight-overrides.css. */}
    <button type="button" class="arrow rotate" data-rotate aria-controls="ask-stack" aria-label="Start automatic rotation">
      <Icon name="lucide:play" class="i-play" />
      <Icon name="lucide:pause" class="i-pause" />
    </button>
    <button type="button" class="arrow" data-prev aria-controls="ask-stack" aria-label="Previous example">
      <Icon name="lucide:chevron-left" />
    </button>
    <div class="dots">
      {examples.map((e, i) => (
        <button type="button" class="dot" data-dot aria-controls="ask-stack" aria-label={`Show example ${i + 1} of ${examples.length}: ${e.tab}`} aria-current={i === 0 ? 'true' : 'false'}></button>
      ))}
    </div>
    <button type="button" class="arrow" data-next aria-controls="ask-stack" aria-label="Next example">
      <Icon name="lucide:chevron-right" />
    </button>
  </div>

  <p class="foot sr sr-d4">
    <a class="more" href={`${base}/papers/explorer/`}>Open the Papers Explorer →</a>
    {/* Beta sits next to the corpus date because the two qualify different things and a
        reader needs both: the date bounds WHEN this was true, the status bounds HOW
        settled it is. Deliberately quiet, because the risk it flags is small — the counts
        and metadata are firm and a placement is a real claim about the paper; the
        re-verification against full texts tightens precision rather than establishing
        trust. Agents get the same calibration via `status` / `placementNote` in
        matrix.json. */}
    <span class="dated"><span class="beta">beta</span>Corpus dated {matrix.corpusDate}</span>
  </p>
</section>

<script>
  /**
   * Carousel controller. Progressive enhancement in the strict sense: without this the
   * first card is already rendered and readable, the accent bar is a static edge, and the
   * controls are inert buttons rather than broken ones.
   */
  const root = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>('.ask');
  const pills = [...(root?.querySelectorAll<HTMLButtonElement>('[data-pill]') ?? [])];
  const cards = [...(root?.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('[data-card]') ?? [])];
  const dots = [...(root?.querySelectorAll<HTMLButtonElement>('[data-dot]') ?? [])];

  if (root && cards.length > 1) {
    const prog = root.querySelector<HTMLElement>('[data-prog]')!;
    const live = root.querySelector<HTMLElement>('[data-live]')!;
    const stack = root.querySelector<HTMLElement>('.stack')!;
    const rotate = root.querySelector<HTMLButtonElement>('[data-rotate]')!;
    const reduce = window.matchMedia('(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)');

    // Rotation is BOUNDED: three passes over the set, then it comes to rest back on the
    // first card. Counted in COMPLETED timed dwells, which is exactly what `animationend`
    // fires once per, and derived from the card count rather than typed so that adding a
    // fifth card does not silently turn three passes into two and a bit.
    const DWELLS_PER_CARD = 3;
    const budget = cards.length * DWELLS_PER_CARD;

    let at = 0;
    let completed = 0;
    // `armed` is whether the clock is running at all; `hovering` and `focused` are the two
    // independent reasons to hold it. They used to share one inline style, so whichever
    // ENDED last won: a keyboard user holding focus in the band had the rotation resume
    // under them the moment a passing mouse left the section, and blurring a pill restarted
    // it with the pointer still inside. Two booleans and one place that reads both.
    let armed = false;
    let hovering = false;
    let focused = false;
    // An explicit start outranks the hold that pressing the start button itself creates.
    // Nobody can activate a control inside the band without the pointer or the focus being
    // inside the band, so `hovering || focused` is ALWAYS true at that moment: without this
    // the button armed the rotation and froze it at scaleX(0) in the same breath, and a
    // reader pressed play and watched nothing happen — by mouse and by keyboard alike.
    // Cleared once BOTH holds have left rather than on the first of the two, because a mouse
    // click also takes focus, so clearing it on `pointerleave` puts the freeze straight back.
    let overrideHold = false;
    // Held here rather than beside its own construction because both stopping and starting
    // the rotation have to be able to cancel an arming that has not happened yet.
    let io: IntersectionObserver | null = null;

    const syncHold = () => {
      const held = hovering || focused;
      if (!held) overrideHold = false;
      prog.style.animationPlayState = held && !overrideHold ? 'paused' : '';
    };

    const setArmed = (on: boolean) => {
      armed = on;
      root.toggleAttribute('data-rotating', on);
      rotate.classList.toggle('is-playing', on);
      rotate.setAttribute('aria-label', on ? 'Stop automatic rotation' : 'Start automatic rotation');
      if (!on) prog.classList.remove('run');
    };

    const restart = () => {
      prog.classList.remove('run');
      if (!armed) return;
      // The class change only takes effect once the removal has been flushed; without the
      // forced reflow the animation is never re-run and the carousel stalls after one card.
      void prog.offsetWidth;
      prog.classList.add('run');
      syncHold(); // re-assert a hold that was in force across the restart
    };

    /**
     * `byUser` separates a change the reader asked for from one the clock made. It decides
     * whether the live region speaks — not whether the card animates, which it always does.
     * Those two were one flag, which is how the timer came to announce itself every 16
     * seconds to anyone reading anywhere on the homepage.
     */
    const show = (n: number, byUser: boolean) => {
      at = (n + cards.length) % cards.length;
      cards.forEach((c, i) => {
        if (i === at) c.removeAttribute('data-off');
        else c.setAttribute('data-off', '');
        // Never `hidden`: display:none removes the card from grid sizing and the frame
        // would collapse to the active card's height, jumping on every change.
        c.classList.toggle('is-enter', i === at);
      });
      pills.forEach((p, i) => p.setAttribute('aria-pressed', String(i === at)));
      dots.forEach((d, i) => d.setAttribute('aria-current', String(i === at)));
      if (byUser) live.textContent = `${pills[at]?.textContent?.trim() ?? ''}: example ${at + 1} of ${cards.length}`;
      restart();
    };

    const startRotation = () => {
      // The observer's only job is the FIRST arming, so an explicit start makes it redundant
      // — and leaving it connected lets it fire a moment later and reset the count under a
      // reader who had just started the rotation themselves.
      io?.disconnect();
      io = null;
      completed = 0;
      overrideHold = true;
      setArmed(true);
      restart();
    };
    const stopRotation = () => {
      // Cancels a PENDING arm as well as a running one. Rotation starts when the band is
      // scrolled to, so a reader who reaches the band and picks a card in the same movement
      // could otherwise have the observer start rotating a moment later — the exact
      // interruption that stopping on first interaction exists to prevent. Narrow window,
      // but the whole invariant is "a deliberate move ends it", and a race that breaks it
      // occasionally is not a weaker version of that rule, it is not that rule.
      io?.disconnect();
      io = null;
      if (armed) setArmed(false);
    };

    prog.addEventListener('animationend', () => {
      if (!armed) return;
      completed += 1;
      // `completed` counts dwells that have FINISHED, so it starts at 0 and this is the only
      // place it moves. That base is the whole of the accounting, and it is easy to get
      // wrong in a way nothing shows: seeding it at 1 to mean "a dwell is in progress" costs
      // the trailing card a turn, giving [3,3,3,2] and resting on the last card instead of
      // [3,3,3,3] resting on the first. Whether this check runs before or after the advance
      // makes no difference to either — it was checked by simulating all four combinations,
      // not reasoned about — so it sits first, where it saves `show` a pointless restart.
      if (completed >= budget) stopRotation();
      show(at + 1, false);
    });

    // Any deliberate move stops the rotation for good: once a reader has expressed intent,
    // moving the content out from under them again is hostile. The rotate button is the one
    // control that can also start it, which is what makes it a mechanism rather than a sign.
    pills.forEach((p, i) => p.addEventListener('click', () => { stopRotation(); show(i, true); }));
    dots.forEach((d, i) => d.addEventListener('click', () => { stopRotation(); show(i, true); }));
    root.querySelector('[data-prev]')?.addEventListener('click', () => { stopRotation(); show(at - 1, true); });
    root.querySelector('[data-next]')?.addEventListener('click', () => { stopRotation(); show(at + 1, true); });
    rotate.addEventListener('click', () => { if (armed) stopRotation(); else startRotation(); });

    root.addEventListener('pointerenter', () => { hovering = true; syncHold(); });
    root.addEventListener('pointerleave', () => { hovering = false; syncHold(); });
    root.addEventListener('focusin', () => { focused = true; syncHold(); });
    root.addEventListener('focusout', (e) => {
      // `focusout` fires before the incoming element takes focus, and `relatedTarget` is
      // where it is going. Moving between two controls inside the band is not leaving it.
      if (e.relatedTarget instanceof Node && root.contains(e.relatedTarget)) return;
      focused = false;
      syncHold();
    });

    reduce.addEventListener('change', () => { if (reduce.matches) stopRotation(); });

    setArmed(false); // establishes the button's rendered state; the observer below arms it

    if (!reduce.matches) {
      // The clock used to start here, at parse time, with the band several screens below
      // the fold: a reader who scrolled down arrived at whatever card the timer had reached
      // behind a part-drawn progress bar, and never saw the first one. Start it when the
      // card frame is first on screen instead.
      //
      // Observing the STACK rather than the section, at threshold 0. A tall section on a
      // short viewport can never reach a fractional threshold, which is a failure that only
      // shows up on the phones least able to report it.
      if ('IntersectionObserver' in window) {
        io = new IntersectionObserver((entries) => {
          if (!entries.some((e) => e.isIntersecting)) return;
          io?.disconnect();
          startRotation();
        }, { threshold: 0 });
        io.observe(stack);
      } else {
        startRotation();
      }
    }
  }
</script>

<style>
  .ask { margin: var(--caail-section-gap) 0 0; }

  /* Centred header, matching the reference band. The measure is constrained so the lede
     wraps to two lines rather than one very long one. */
  .hd { text-align: center; max-width: 42rem; margin: 0 auto var(--caail-space-6); }
  .eyebrow {
    display: inline-flex; align-items: center; gap: .55rem;
    font-size: var(--caail-fs-xs); letter-spacing: .18em; text-transform: uppercase;
    font-weight: 600; color: var(--caail-muted); margin: 0 0 var(--caail-space-3);
  }
  .bar { display: block; width: 2px; height: .85rem; background: var(--caail-link); border-radius: 2px; }
  .ask h2 { font-size: var(--caail-fs-h2); line-height: 1.1; letter-spacing: -.02em; color: var(--caail-primary); margin: 0 0 var(--caail-space-3); text-wrap: balance; }
  .lede { font-size: var(--caail-fs-lg); color: var(--caail-ink); margin: 0 0 var(--caail-space-2); text-wrap: pretty; }
  .sub { font-size: var(--caail-fs-sm); color: var(--caail-muted); margin: 0; text-wrap: pretty; }

  /* Visually hidden but present for assistive tech. Not `visibility`/`display`, either of
     which would take it out of the a11y tree and silence the live region entirely. */
  .vh {
    position: absolute; width: 1px; height: 1px; overflow: hidden;
    clip-path: inset(50%); white-space: nowrap; margin: -1px; padding: 0; border: 0;
  }

  /* Icon pills, centred. Active is filled rather than outlined so the connection to the
     card's accent edge below is unmistakable. */
  .pills { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; justify-content: center; gap: var(--caail-space-2); margin: 0 0 var(--caail-space-6); }
  .pill {
    display: inline-flex; align-items: center; gap: .4rem;
    font: inherit; font-size: var(--caail-fs-sm); font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--caail-muted); background: var(--caail-bg);
    border: 1px solid var(--caail-border); border-radius: 999px;
    padding: .45rem 1rem; cursor: pointer;
    transition: color .15s ease-out, border-color .15s ease-out, background .15s ease-out;
  }
  .pill [data-icon] { width: 1.05rem; height: 1.05rem; }
  .pill:hover { color: var(--caail-link); border-color: var(--caail-link); }
  .pill[aria-pressed='true'] {
    color: var(--caail-link);
    border-color: var(--caail-link);
    background: color-mix(in oklab, var(--caail-link) 9%, transparent);
  }

  /* THE STACK. Every card occupies the same grid cell, so the frame is exactly as tall as
     the tallest card — no reserved height to measure, and none to go stale when the copy
     is edited. The previous `min-height: 37rem` was measured against four panels of
     524/531/549/579px and still left ~115px of void under the shortest. */
  .stack {
    position: relative; display: grid;
    border: 1px solid var(--caail-border); border-radius: 12px;
    overflow: hidden; background: var(--caail-bg);
  }
  .card { grid-area: 1 / 1; display: flex; flex-direction: column; min-width: 0; }
  /* `visibility`, never `hidden`/`display: none`. A display-none card contributes nothing
     to grid track sizing, so the frame would collapse to the active card and jump on every
     change — which is the entire thing the stack exists to prevent. visibility:hidden
     still sizes the grid while leaving the a11y tree and blocking pointer and focus. */
  .card[data-off] { visibility: hidden; }

  /* Progress bar: inside the radius rather than on the border. A 3px top BORDER met the
     1px side borders at the corners and left a visible notch on each. */
  .prog {
    position: absolute; inset: 0 0 auto 0; z-index: 2; height: 3px;
    background: var(--caail-link); transform-origin: 0 50%;
  }
  .prog.run { animation: prog 16s linear forwards; }
  @keyframes prog { from { transform: scaleX(0); } to { transform: scaleX(1); } }

  /* TRANSFORM ONLY — no opacity, and this one is load-bearing. The old tab fade animated
     opacity and argued it was safe because an audit never clicks a tab. Auto-advance ends
     that argument: axe computes contrast through opacity, and a card sampled mid-fade
     reports a bogus violation against the Lighthouse ≥0.90 deploy gate. Staggering the
     three regions gives the change plenty of legibility without it. */
  .card.is-enter > * { animation: card-in .5s cubic-bezier(.16, 1, .3, 1) both; }
  .card.is-enter .csrc { animation-delay: .06s; }
  .card.is-enter .cbody { animation-delay: .12s; }
  @keyframes card-in { from { transform: translateY(14px); } to { transform: none; } }

  /* Tinted, rule-separated regions, as the reference does. This is also what stops the
     slack under a short card reading as a hole: the leftover falls inside a bounded body
     panel rather than inside one undifferentiated box. */
  .chead { padding: var(--caail-space-6); background: var(--caail-surface); border-bottom: 1px solid var(--caail-border); }
  .csrc { display: flex; align-items: center; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: .5rem; padding: var(--caail-space-4) var(--caail-space-6); border-bottom: 1px solid var(--caail-border); font-size: var(--caail-fs-sm); color: var(--caail-muted); }
  .cbody { display: flex; flex-direction: column; padding: var(--caail-space-6); flex: 1; }
  /* Absorbs the stack's slack, so the closing line sits at the foot of a short card
     rather than leaving the void beneath it. */
  .cbody .tail { margin-top: auto; padding-top: var(--caail-space-5); }

  /* Prev / dots / next. The pills name the examples; these give position and a
     "there is more" affordance at the bottom of a long card, where the pills are
     already off screen. */
  .ctrls { display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; gap: var(--caail-space-4); margin: var(--caail-space-5) 0 0; }
  .arrow {
    display: inline-flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center;
    width: 2.25rem; height: 2.25rem; padding: 0; cursor: pointer;
    color: var(--caail-muted); background: var(--caail-bg);
    border: 1px solid var(--caail-border); border-radius: 999px;
    transition: color .15s ease-out, border-color .15s ease-out;
  }
  .arrow [data-icon] { width: 1.05rem; height: 1.05rem; }
  .arrow:hover { color: var(--caail-ink); border-color: var(--caail-link); }

  /* Icons swapped by `display`, so exactly one is ever laid out and the button cannot
     change size across the swap. Rendered state is "stopped", which is true at load. */
  .rotate .i-play, .rotate .i-pause { display: none; }
  .rotate:not(.is-playing) .i-play { display: block; }
  .rotate.is-playing .i-pause { display: block; }
  /* While it is rotating, the control is the live thing on the row rather than another
     grey affordance — it is the one a reader is looking for when they want it to stop. */
  .rotate.is-playing { color: var(--caail-link); border-color: var(--caail-link); }

  .dots { display: flex; align-items: center; gap: .4rem; }
  /* The dot's BOX never changes size; only the pill drawn inside it does.
     This used to transition `width` from .5rem to 1.1rem, which is a layout property: every
     frame of every change re-laid-out the row, and the dots either side slid to make room.
     `clip-path` paints the same pill without touching layout, and 27% of 1.1rem leaves
     .5rem showing, so the inactive dot is the size it always was. The constant 1.1rem box
     also gives the hit target somewhere to grow from — see the expander below.
     Interpolation sampled mid-transition in Chromium and Firefox; WebKit is not installed
     here and is unverified. The risk that leaves is bounded and cosmetic: the box is a
     constant width whether or not the clip animates, so an engine that refused to
     interpolate would snap the indicator, not move the row. */
  .dot {
    position: relative;
    width: 1.1rem; height: .5rem; padding: 0; cursor: pointer;
    border: 0; background: none;
  }
  .dot::before {
    content: ''; display: block; width: 100%; height: 100%;
    border-radius: 999px; background: var(--caail-border);
    clip-path: inset(0 27% 0 27% round 999px);
    transition: clip-path .2s ease-out, background .2s ease-out;
  }
  .dot:hover::before { background: var(--caail-muted); }
  .dot[aria-current='true']::before {
    clip-path: inset(0 0 0 0 round 999px);
    background: var(--caail-link);
  }
